#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096538 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096731 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f354f758-3745-5cee-9a4b-a0878c2c8578" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Area Bounded by a Parametric Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "85879186-0866-5159-905b-65d043681231" :question/math-academy-id "e-604" :question/problem "![](images/e9/e933e484b931ee54b345a8e7af3b0e44f80a8b019e3e401007ad4987192e33be.png)\n\nFind the area bounded between the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx = t^2 - 1, \\qquad y = t^3 - 4t\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis for $t \\in [-2,0].$" :question/worked-solution "The unsigned area bounded by the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx = x(t), \\qquad\ny = y(t), \\qquad\nt_1 \\leq t \\leq t_2\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis is given by the absolute value of the integral\n\n$$\n\\int_{t_1}^{t_2} y \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t} \\:\\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-1) \\\\  & =2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the integral expression\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{-2}^{0}({t}^{3}-4t)(2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{-2}^{0}{t}^{2}({t}^{2}-4)\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that this integral will give us a negative number since $y\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t} \\leq 0$ for all $t \\in [-2,0].$\n\nTherefore, the area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =-2{\\int }_{-2}^{0}{t}^{2}({t}^{2}-4)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{-2}^{0}{t}^{2}(4-{t}^{2})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{-2}^{0}(4{t}^{2}-{t}^{4})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{-2}^{0}{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t-2{\\int }_{-2}^{0}{t}^{4}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}\\,{|}_{-2}^{0}-2\\cdot \\frac{{t}^{5}}{5}\\,{|}_{-2}^{0} \\\\  & =8(\\frac{{0}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{(-2)^{3}}{3})-2(\\frac{{0}^{5}}{5}-\\frac{(-2)^{5}}{5}) \\\\  & =8(\\frac{8}{3})-2(\\frac{32}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{64}{3}-\\frac{64}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{128}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096561 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096758 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f20da72e-5a93-5a4e-be39-d412bd8fee86" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integral Expression for the Area Bounded by a Parametric Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "95cdc31d-c3f2-50b6-9447-b7317af775f9" :question/math-academy-id "e-7412" :question/problem "![](images/3d/3dd84b2e84056a4e6c71f7b76534f5a6f1a46132ea5da5ed8edb2a514b9ea6b4.png)\n\nFind an integral expression for the area bounded between the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx = e^{-t^2}, \\qquad y = t^3,\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis for $0 \\leq t \\leq \\sqrt{2} \\,?$" :question/worked-solution "The unsigned area bounded by the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx = x(t), \\qquad\ny = y(t), \\qquad\nt_1 \\leq t \\leq t_2\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis is given by the absolute value of the integral\n\n$$\n\\int_{t_1}^{t_2} y \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t} \\:\\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{-{t}^{2}}) \\\\  & =-2t{e}^{-{t}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the integral expression\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}{t}^{3}(-2t{e}^{-{t}^{2}})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}{t}^{4}{e}^{-{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that this integral will give us a negative number since $y\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t} \\leq 0$ for all $t \\in [0, \\sqrt{2}].$\n\nTherefore, the area is given by\n\n$$\nA = -\\left( -2 \\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}} t^4 e^{-t^2} \\, \\textrm{d}t \\right) = 2 \\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}} t^4 e^{-t^2} \\, \\textrm{d}t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533985 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096731 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f354f758-3745-5cee-9a4b-a0878c2c8578" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Area Bounded by a Parametric Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0780ca6a-c7cd-5c07-86b1-25dd14713907" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{34}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533153} {:answer/id #uuid "1c30baac-673c-57e4-bb6d-271c0fec004e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533206} {:answer/id #uuid "41ebf8a0-45bd-57a0-9e78-b6a77b454d60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{64}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533294} {:answer/id #uuid "4fc9aebc-6c01-51de-a0b0-7677f18cc47d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{32}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533331} {:answer/id #uuid "f6b49b5a-7449-5ccb-b7ed-94a7b282e585" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{64}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533744}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0780ca6a-c7cd-5c07-86b1-25dd14713907" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{34}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533153} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a3d8b06-7a62-51cc-b468-4393734ce6fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533842}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "26258e04-30b1-50d5-abea-f32adfbd6868" :question/math-academy-id "q-10208" :question/problem "![](images/b0/b0d11e89592cad64ebbeb72def6ca0a6c5880a9a54f82b394d7857e142c05edf.png)\n\nFind the area bounded between the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx={t}^{3}-4t,\\,y={t}^{2}-1\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis for $t\\in [0,1].$" :question/worked-solution "The unsigned area bounded by the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=x(t),\\,y=y(t),\\,{t}_{1}\\le t\\le {t}_{2}\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis is given by the absolute value of the integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-4t) \\\\  & =3{t}^{2}-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the integral expression\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}-1)(3{t}^{2}-4)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(3{t}^{4}-7{t}^{2}+4)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that this integral will give us a positive number since $y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\ge 0$ for all $t\\in [0,1].$\n\nTherefore, the area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(3{t}^{4}-7{t}^{2}+4)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{4}\\,\\text{d}t-7{\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t+4{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3\\cdot {\\frac{{t}^{5}}{5}\\,|}_{0}^{1}-7\\cdot {\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}\\,|}_{0}^{1}+4\\cdot t\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =3(\\frac{1}{5}-0)-7(\\frac{1}{3}-0)+4(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}-\\frac{7}{3}+4 \\\\  & =\\frac{34}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534040 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096731 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f354f758-3745-5cee-9a4b-a0878c2c8578" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Area Bounded by a Parametric Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28b4ed19-b0a4-543e-83a7-51916dd3fa22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{256}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533242} {:answer/id #uuid "530f3b53-a178-5021-8567-be1e8771a0d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{64}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533338} {:answer/id #uuid "5d6d8667-2807-5f6b-8f49-adf93ef75072" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{128}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533352} {:answer/id #uuid "6cc00630-1342-5bbe-ab51-a97a4c091b46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{128}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533393} {:answer/id #uuid "d720d6b2-51cb-5455-acd7-e39b10f0feef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{512}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533663}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "28b4ed19-b0a4-543e-83a7-51916dd3fa22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{256}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533242} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ba96fc9-73a2-5b2b-9e01-ee5a33e187b7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533816}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9ef740c3-9ce9-5fcc-84b0-6a5ac9ae7a65" :question/math-academy-id "q-10214" :question/problem "![](images/16/1628d71e973666b1b79aa5e86a9fa4b7cc8fffec93778bde92db7b3600bd13f5.png)\n\nFind the area enclosed by the curve (shown above) defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx={t}^{2}-4,\\,y={t}^{3}-4t,\\,t\\in (-2,2).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The unsigned area bounded by the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=x(t),\\,y=y(t),\\,{t}_{1}\\le t\\le {t}_{2}\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis is given by the absolute value of the integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-4)=2t.\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the integral expression\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}({t}^{3}-4t)\\cdot 2t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{-2}^{2}({t}^{4}-4{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that this integral will give us a negative number since $y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\le 0$ for all $t\\in (-2,2).$\n\nTherefore, the area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =2{\\int }_{-2}^{2}({t}^{4}-4{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\cdot {\\frac{{t}^{5}}{5}|}_{-2}^{2}-8\\cdot {\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}|}_{-2}^{2} \\\\  & =2(\\frac{32}{5}+\\frac{32}{5})-8(\\frac{8}{3}+\\frac{8}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{128}{5}-\\frac{128}{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{256}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required area is $|-\\frac{256}{15}|=\\frac{256}{15}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097133 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096758 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f20da72e-5a93-5a4e-be39-d412bd8fee86" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integral Expression for the Area Bounded by a Parametric Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b7ecc71-9a38-5790-8068-af238cc70091" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{2}}t({t}^{3}-1)({t}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533161} {:answer/id #uuid "4e769969-8957-55a2-b6ab-5d02f6fc2146" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{2}}t({t}^{3}-{t}^{2})({t}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533327} {:answer/id #uuid "ad138fd0-cf17-507e-a68b-3714864985e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{2}}({t}^{3}-t)({t}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533554} {:answer/id #uuid "bcaf9820-1bec-5a03-9a35-ed2b702da002" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{2}}({t}^{3}-1)({t}^{2}-t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533595} {:answer/id #uuid "dc32fe65-23f4-585b-b44d-bc65d429edc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{2}}({t}^{3}-t)({t}^{2}-t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533677}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b7ecc71-9a38-5790-8068-af238cc70091" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{2}}t({t}^{3}-1)({t}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533161} :answer-field/id #uuid "eeb17fdb-d008-5d2a-befe-3afdce935947" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094771}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ad65213f-7896-5eb3-8900-a92c0319ea40" :question/math-academy-id "q-15688" :question/problem "![](images/3b/3bde5b93aa28654977fdb55b0b2808fde2fb2f54ba33a6e9a2a4281b6966682f.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the area bounded between the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx={t}^{4}-2{t}^{2},\\,y={t}^{3}-1\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis for $t\\in [1,\\sqrt{2}]?$" :question/worked-solution "The unsigned area bounded by the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=x(t),\\,y=y(t),\\,{t}_{1}\\le t\\le {t}_{2}\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis is given by the absolute value of the integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{4}-2{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =4{t}^{3}-4t \\\\  & =4t({t}^{2}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the integral expression\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{2}}({t}^{3}-1)4t({t}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{2}}t({t}^{3}-1)({t}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that this integral will give us a positive number since $y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\ge 0$ for all $t\\in [1,\\sqrt{2}].$\n\nTherefore, the area is given by\n\n$$\nA=4{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{2}}t({t}^{3}-1)({t}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534014 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096758 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f20da72e-5a93-5a4e-be39-d412bd8fee86" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integral Expression for the Area Bounded by a Parametric Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "96ccd538-a21d-543b-84e0-95a8be106823" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\sqrt{3})\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533507} {:answer/id #uuid "a6c9a372-2f34-5f10-87bb-315c867896e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{2\\pi /3}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\sqrt{3})\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533541} {:answer/id #uuid "a97f4b2f-c728-5cb6-86b1-cfb0247b8d9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\sqrt{3})\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533548} {:answer/id #uuid "b0d8a8f7-9728-574b-a5bf-93dc33ae91c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi /3}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\sqrt{3})\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533566} {:answer/id #uuid "b5ed7e91-3d72-56b5-ab62-5c3e1bc6ce84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{\\int }_{-\\pi /3}^{\\pi /3}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\sqrt{3})\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533582}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a6c9a372-2f34-5f10-87bb-315c867896e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{2\\pi /3}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\sqrt{3})\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533541} :answer-field/id #uuid "4bc41e98-bdd4-510c-bb8f-f153e9da4aad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533844}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "65750eba-d000-52c0-9873-7b35cf42c92d" :question/math-academy-id "q-15697" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the area bounded between the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\sqrt{3}\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis for $t\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{2\\pi }{3}]?$" :question/worked-solution "The unsigned area bounded by the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=x(t),\\,y=y(t),\\,{t}_{1}\\le t\\le {t}_{2}\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis is given by the absolute value of the integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the integral expression\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{2\\pi /3}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\sqrt{3})(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{2\\pi /3}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\sqrt{3})\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that this integral will give us a negative number since $y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\le 0$ for all $t\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{2\\pi }{3}].$\n\nTherefore, the area is given by\n\n$$\nA=-(-3{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{2\\pi /3}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\sqrt{3})\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t)=3{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{2\\pi /3}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\sqrt{3})\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534021 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096758 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f20da72e-5a93-5a4e-be39-d412bd8fee86" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integral Expression for the Area Bounded by a Parametric Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "68b23a53-a860-5499-be46-e095e504ab4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533386} {:answer/id #uuid "7d09ac4b-ab8c-5486-8d70-86fa00fbd80f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533436} {:answer/id #uuid "9ab8b9c7-33ef-5efc-9e7b-b5a66785ef0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533515} {:answer/id #uuid "a5ec7899-b1aa-5e69-833b-85fe4f8f90c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}t(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533539} {:answer/id #uuid "dc79816e-db4c-5c6c-9732-8d3c834824bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}{e}^{-t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533680}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a5ec7899-b1aa-5e69-833b-85fe4f8f90c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}t(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533539} :answer-field/id #uuid "282f87a1-fbff-540e-9588-15f337435902" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533811}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "758e2016-fc3b-5023-8258-63b37b29bb3a" :question/math-academy-id "q-15702" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the area bounded between the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx={e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,y=t{e}^{t}\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis for $t\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0]?$" :question/worked-solution "The unsigned area bounded by the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=x(t),\\,y=y(t),\\,{t}_{1}\\le t\\le {t}_{2}\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis is given by the absolute value of the integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =-{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & ={e}^{-t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the integral expression\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}t{e}^{t}\\cdot {e}^{-t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}t(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that this integral will give us a negative number since $y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\le 0$ for all $t\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0].$\n\nTherefore, the area is given by\n\n$$\nA=-{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}t(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}t(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534099 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096758 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f20da72e-5a93-5a4e-be39-d412bd8fee86" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integral Expression for the Area Bounded by a Parametric Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05e68831-30d3-5adb-84ff-01959317c4c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533149} {:answer/id #uuid "35655e85-01d4-5c73-bf09-db2bf24b1303" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{ln}^{2}⁡(t+1){sec}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533264} {:answer/id #uuid "b62d29f0-effc-5844-9775-69af319ada0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533583} {:answer/id #uuid "bdff80fb-f43a-5cf0-8e42-04896271cf0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{ln}^{2}⁡(t+1)\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533597} {:answer/id #uuid "bfbcdd0a-4848-5292-b557-339794df61df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1){sec}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533602}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "35655e85-01d4-5c73-bf09-db2bf24b1303" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{ln}^{2}⁡(t+1){sec}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533264} :answer-field/id #uuid "b75b49fc-3a38-5d99-88f4-84100e658eca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533914}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fe120378-20db-5fc9-9e0d-984daf992ad4" :question/math-academy-id "q-15703" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the area bounded between the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=-\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y={ln}^{2}⁡(t+1)\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis for $t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{4}]?$" :question/worked-solution "The unsigned area bounded by the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=x(t),\\,y=y(t),\\,{t}_{1}\\le t\\le {t}_{2}\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis is given by the absolute value of the integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{tan}⁡t) \\\\  & =-{sec}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the integral expression\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{ln}^{2}⁡(t+1)(-{sec}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{ln}^{2}⁡(t+1){sec}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that this integral will give us a negative number since $y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\le 0$ for all $t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{4}].$\n\nTherefore, the area is given by\n\n$$\nA=-(-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{ln}^{2}⁡(t+1){sec}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t)={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{ln}^{2}⁡(t+1){sec}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533987 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096758 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f20da72e-5a93-5a4e-be39-d412bd8fee86" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integral Expression for the Area Bounded by a Parametric Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2be295bf-ab71-5c18-a2d8-e9a46caf6c7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533248} {:answer/id #uuid "48e2bc52-9d10-5087-adba-a5e457f49069" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533309} {:answer/id #uuid "4a25f513-1d63-5b75-9076-d74d78ecfa96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533313} {:answer/id #uuid "b2047660-6762-59c0-8c44-62f24bd512e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533569} {:answer/id #uuid "d1bf2e47-4e6c-5652-b8c3-c4940c8da459" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533650}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d1bf2e47-4e6c-5652-b8c3-c4940c8da459" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533650} :answer-field/id #uuid "272dc5e4-a05a-579c-866f-fc7b62dce493" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533810}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "28dbcab1-e3b5-5a8b-9a9c-02483c0b7b4f" :question/math-academy-id "q-21261" :question/problem "![](images/a8/a886f775367f244f78087935cb717b988d85f1714b2cae7ce3e4ba2525ef178d.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the area bounded between the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,y=t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis for $t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]?$" :question/worked-solution "The unsigned area bounded by the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=x(t),\\,y=y(t),\\,{t}_{1}\\le t\\le {t}_{2}\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis is given by the absolute value of the integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the integral expression\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that this integral will give us a positive number since $y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\ge 0$ for all $t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$\n\nTherefore, the area is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534025 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096731 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f354f758-3745-5cee-9a4b-a0878c2c8578" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Area Bounded by a Parametric Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2def6620-57ba-5d95-9beb-b3aeecd0552a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533251} {:answer/id #uuid "44a447b9-c086-5162-9309-62ffa3961404" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533300} {:answer/id #uuid "c5d87b82-389c-5639-bd59-8e893e25e124" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533615} {:answer/id #uuid "db98e4f8-00f3-5545-84b9-5d5a5f5d9362" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533675} {:answer/id #uuid "f6553b7c-f441-5101-85f6-7f1945901c38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533743}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c5d87b82-389c-5639-bd59-8e893e25e124" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533615} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f1a9067-bb2d-5d4e-be8a-b58dfed87758" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533820}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7fa9ec9c-84e1-5ae9-97e5-a5fa49b7d98b" :question/math-academy-id "q-21273" :question/problem "![](images/39/399578c2a4d43e64be3f811ab0fdf0f5a62456c29d77dc3a822cc0ecea733c77.png)\n\nFind the area bounded between the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=2{t}^{2},\\,y=2t\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis for $0\\le t\\le 1.$" :question/worked-solution "The unsigned area bounded by the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=x(t),\\,y=y(t),\\,{t}_{1}\\le t\\le {t}_{2}\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis is given by the absolute value of the integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =4t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the integral expression\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2t(4t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that this integral will give us a positive number since $y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\ge 0$ for all $t\\in [0,1].$\n\nTherefore, the area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =8{\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8\\cdot {\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}\\,|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =8(\\frac{1}{3}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534049 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096731 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f354f758-3745-5cee-9a4b-a0878c2c8578" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Area Bounded by a Parametric Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "872be18b-4511-5349-a1db-41e23ea8fdb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533460}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "872be18b-4511-5349-a1db-41e23ea8fdb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533460} :answer-field/id #uuid "655bb155-c367-5d19-b7ad-147108ed79e4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533859}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ae8f5be4-7a5b-5146-8905-098ca73c2947" :question/math-academy-id "q-351633" :question/problem "![](images/09/0928371c060dc402198b610e7118fdcff68cd0e10c54df43931ff82d2a2697b4.png)\n\nFind the area $A$ bounded between the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=t,\\,y=t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis for $t\\in [\\pi ,2\\pi ].$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The unsigned area bounded by the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=x(t),\\,y=y(t),\\,{t}_{1}\\le t\\le {t}_{2}\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis is given by the absolute value of the integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the integral expression\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that this integral will give us a negative number since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\le 0$ for all $t\\in [\\pi ,2\\pi ]$ and $t\\ge 0.$\n\nTo solve the above integral, we use integration by parts. We set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=t & ⇒ & {u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & ⇒ & v=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & =-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{|}_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }+{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{|}_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }+{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t| \\\\  & =|\\,-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{|}_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }+{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t\\,| \\\\  & =|\\,-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{|}_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{|}_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }\\,| \\\\  & =|\\,-(2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\pi -\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi )+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi )\\,| \\\\  & =|\\,-(2\\pi (1)-\\pi (-1))\\,| \\\\  & =3\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534062 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096731 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f354f758-3745-5cee-9a4b-a0878c2c8578" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Area Bounded by a Parametric Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c197d1b-3113-566d-b58d-a4d97b59479e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\ln 2-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533164}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c197d1b-3113-566d-b58d-a4d97b59479e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\ln 2-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533164} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e6ff139-6e60-549e-b50b-521438ae9cc8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533800}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c3acb4f5-8405-55f7-be88-53d3f71a5599" :question/math-academy-id "q-351638" :question/problem "![](images/60/60f01450ff74239f3f9bedcf9dfc4445f9c557bb52584ee79a0ba61b0d8bb6f7.png)\n\nFind the area bounded between the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=t,\\,y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis for $t\\in [0,1].$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The unsigned area bounded by the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=x(t),\\,y=y(t),\\,{t}_{1}\\le t\\le {t}_{2}\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis is given by the absolute value of the integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the integral expression\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that this integral will give us a positive number since $y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\ge 0$ for all $t\\in [0,1].$\n\nTherefore, the area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(t+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)-(t+1)\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-2)-(1\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡1-1) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-2-(0-1) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
